MLflow integration tests I recommend

I would start with 5 integration tests.

1. Run setup → tags → parameters → metrics

Purpose: Verify that a real MLflow run receives all the expected metadata.

setup_experiment()
       ↓
start_run()
       ↓
set_run_tags()
       ↓
log_parameters()
       ↓
log_metrics()
       ↓
verify run metadata

This verifies that your MLflow utilities work together.

2. Input data → lineage → MLflow run

This is particularly important for your project.

load input
    ↓
start MLflow run
    ↓
set_source_lineage_tags()
    ↓
log_input_dataset()
    ↓
log_artifact(input.xlsx)
    ↓
verify lineage

You want to prove that the MLflow run contains enough information to answer:

Which dataset/file produced this run?

3. Model logging → validation → registration

This is probably your highest-priority MLflow integration test.

MLflow run
   ↓
log_transformer_model()
   ↓
validate model
   ↓
register_model()
   ↓
model version created
   ↓
model version tags

Verify things such as:

model was logged
registered model exists
correct registered name
correct version
parent_run_id
dataset_version
validation_status
registration status

This connects functionality you currently test separately.

4. Evaluation → composite score → promotion → MLflow metadata

This should connect your evaluation and promotion_utils with MLflow.

Evaluation results
       ↓
add_quality_labels()
       ↓
calculate_composite_score()
       ↓
promote_models()
       ↓
MLflow metrics/tags
       ↓
Champion / Challenger

For example, if:

model_1 = 0.90
model_2 = 0.80

the integration test should verify that the MLflow metadata ultimately reflects:

model_1 → Champion
model_2 → Challenger
5. Incumbent Champion → new evaluation → promotion decision

This is especially important because you have:

get_incumbent_champion()

and:

promote_models(
    eval_df,
    incumbent_champion_score=...
)

Integration flow:

Existing MLflow Model
        ↓
get_incumbent_champion()
        ↓
existing Champion score
        ↓
new evaluation
        ↓
composite score
        ↓
compare against incumbent
        ↓
promotion decision

Test both:

Candidate wins

incumbent = 0.70
candidate = 0.80
→ new Champion

and

Candidate loses

incumbent = 0.80
candidate = 0.70
→ promotion blocked
I would NOT make this your first integration test

I wouldn't start with:

test_main()

covering the entire mlflow_pipeline.main().

Your main() is doing too many things:

Input
 ↓
filter
 ↓
preprocess
 ↓
summarize
 ↓
MLflow
 ↓
register model
 ↓
evaluation
 ↓
promotion
 ↓
model card

That test will become difficult to debug.

Instead, build the MLflow integration tests around logical lifecycle boundaries.

Your test structure could become
tests/
│
├── unit/
│   ├── test_mlflow_utils.py
│   └── test_promotion_utils.py
│
└── integration/
    ├── test_mlflow_run.py
    ├── test_mlflow_lineage.py
    ├── test_mlflow_model_lifecycle.py
    └── test_mlflow_promotion.py
My priority order
Priority	Integration test
⭐⭐⭐⭐⭐	Model logging → registration → model version metadata
⭐⭐⭐⭐⭐	Evaluation → composite score → Champion/Challenger → MLflow
⭐⭐⭐⭐	Existing Champion → new candidate → promotion decision
⭐⭐⭐⭐	Input → lineage → MLflow run
⭐⭐⭐	Run → tags → parameters → metrics
